Probable root cause

1. There is some node instability evident due to autoscale churn. I think GCP auto scaled down during the upgrade and then scaled them back up.
2. Model upload inside an orchestration took 52 seconds in one case. That one log was 96 seconds until finish. This might have been slowed by other orchestrations that were all uploading at once, but I don't have evidence of them. I also don't have evidence the generatl nodes are under provisioned as initially suspected.
3. There was very likely some output buffering from the Orchestration tasks into their logs which led to some log visibility delay. The typical Python buffer is 8192 bytes and that log above we did capture was only 4470, so it's possible the whole thing was cached until exit at 96 seconds, followed by some transit through the database etc, before it was available to the user.
4. Unrelated: There are some unknown Plateau instances hanging around that we should help them clean up.

Suggested mitigations

1. Dampen scale-down aggressiveness around upgrades (or maintain higher min nodes / longer scale-down delay on key pools) so transient rollout valleys don’t trigger disruptive node turnover.
2. It might help future debugging if Wallaroo added timestamps in our portion (WALLAROO:) of the orchestration logs.
3. Also it would help if customers would add timestamps when printing (eg by using the logging package).
4. Customers who need the logs sooner (at the expense of throughput) can add flush=True or sys.stdout.flush() to their orchestration printing to eliminate the buffering issue.





